.TH PCI_READ 2
.SH NAME
pci_read, pci_write \- PCI configuration space, for drivers (SYS_PCI)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

uint32_t pci_read(uint32_t a);
void pci_write(uint32_t a, uint32_t v);

syscall4(SYS_PCI, address, value, write, 0);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SYS_PCI
reads or (with
.I write
not 0) writes one 32-bit word of a PCI device's configuration space,
through ports 0xCF8 and 0xCFC (x86-64).
.I address
is bus << 16 | device << 11 | function << 8 | register (the register a
multiple of 4). The kernel does the two port accesses together, under a
lock, so drivers scanning the bus at the same time never mix their index
and data.
.PP
Drivers only. user/lib/pci.c builds on it: finding devices, their BARs,
MSI and MSI-X
.RB ( irq_msi (2)).
.SH RETURN VALUE
The word read (0 for a write), or a negative error.
.B pci_read
returns it as a
.BR uint32_t ,
so an error looks like a value: only drivers on x86-64 should call it.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not a driver.
.TP
.B ENOSYS
Not x86-64 (on arm64, PCI configuration space is memory, found in the
device tree and mapped with
.BR map_phys (2)).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irq_msi (2),
.BR map_phys (2),
.BR sys_acpi (2)
